> >just changing the clock crystal.

> Argh!  Please let's stay away from analogies to computers!

That was my point... the analogy between "clock speed" in a computer
and "transmission speed" in a brain is quite broken, and not readily
fixed.  The brain works like a brain, not like an electrical
circuit, a "neural network", a symbolic AI program, or an
instantiation of cognitive psychology.

People have pointed out all sorts of things you could do with
neurons: arranging synapses to form AND gates, putting together R-S
flip-flops, and so forth.  I find this a little silly; the fact that
you make make an eight-bit binary adder out of a dendritic tree does
not mean that a brain would even find such a thing useful, much
less actually contain one.

