Date: Tue, 6 Apr 1993 18:03:32 -0400 (EDT)
From: esr@snark.thyrsus.com (Eric S. Raymond)
Subject: Korzybski's "E-Prime", also NLP

> BTW, does anyone on the list know much about "Neurolinguistic Programming",
> aka NLP?  From what little I've seen it looks like General Semantics
> reborn -- I wonder if its creators & advocates acknowledge a previous
> incarnation of the ideas?

Yes, they do.  Very explicitly, in print, and on numerous occasions.  I think
G & B's first book, "The Structure Of Magic" (TSoM) was dedicated to Korzybski.
They thought of it as a straightforward extension of GS principles, and said so.

(By the way, I should have mentioned GS in the earlier threads about memes
for promoting rationality.  I was very, very lucky --- my maternal grandfather
was one of the early General Semanticists and exposed me to it when I was 12.
I believe that did more to increase my effective intelligence and bullshit-
detection ability than any other single act by anyone in my life.)

TSoM describes a system of psychotherapy founded on the Korzybskian principle
that most dysfunction is either caused or abetted by a mismatch between the
client's semantic map of reality and that reality.  It models what Freudians
and neo-Freudians call "neuroses" as the results of deletions in the client's
semantic map.  It teaches techniques for helping the client restore those
deletions.

This is an extremely general and powerful way of looking at therapy.  One
of the fascinating things about "The Structure of Magic" is the way that
they map various `classical' therapeutic schools into particular classes
of deletions in the client's language.  Blind men looking at an elephant
...and TSoM shows you the elephant.  The sense that you are in the presence
of a sort of Newtonian unification in psychotherapy is intense.

NLP goes beyond TSoM in that it extends the analysis to internal knowledge
representations that aren't language-based, especially sensory memory.  Nor
is it mere theorizing.  NLP is able to deduce some techniques for deducing
and manipulating peoples' mental states that are very effective; sometimes
frighteningly so.  I've gotten good use out of a number of them myself.

NLP is relatively ineffective on disorders that are endogenous; depression,
schizophrenia (yes, I know that isn't ARM-III any more), obsessive/compulsive
behavior, all that jazz.  Thus, it's never going to clear out the mental wards.
But then, none of the competing "talking cures" works worth a damn on those
either.

For the minor functional stuff involved in most therapy and counselling,
though, it has no peer that I know of.  Even its parlor tricks are spooky.
If I'm ever FTF with any of you, ask me to teach you the NLP hack for
telling whether a person can spell English well just by watching his/her
kinesics while he/she mentally spells a difficult one.
-- 
					Eric S. Raymond <esr@snark.thyrsus.com>

