   It's not like this is purely academic; there are people who preserve
   endangered species for a living: Zimbawean villagers do it with their
   very own African elephants (Indian elephants aren't endangered, because
   they've been owned, publicly and privately, for millenia), and Texan
   game ranchers do it with endangered antelope species (one has preserved
   all 27 bloodlines of one kind of antelope) which they raise sustainably
   for exotic steak dinners.  There are more of some of these kinds of
   antelopes in Texas than on their indigenous continent, Africa.

True, but those are species with present economic value.  I was
thinking more of cases like the northern spotted owl, which has no
present value (and sits on top of a lot of valuable timber).  In order
for an investor to be willing to spend money *now* to preserve the
owl, there has to be high assurance that the investor will capture the
benefit of the owl's existance later.  If the time-periods involved
are significantly longer than the time needed to breed a large
population from a few, there is little economic difference between
owning 10 and owning 100,000 of them.  If, 100 years from now, someone
can snatch a few from somewhere, the investor could wind up with no
economic advantage in return for his long investment.

