Subject: Dangers of Emphasizing IQ and IQ Testing

There's a nice but somewhat wordy exploration of these ideas in Martin
Gardner's book "Frames of Mind." He talks about different *types* of
intelligence. Most IQ tests measure a narrow part of intelligence:
verbal recall, word manipulation, mathematical puzzle solving and
speed of solving short problems. There are other types of mathematical
intelligence, and other types of intelligence. There are social and
political intelligences: empathy, the ability to sense others
feelings; political intelligence, the ability to lead and to gather
support; the body intelligences, balance, spatial sense, perception;
the artistic intelligences, musical or artistic ability; mechanical
intelligences, the ability to understand and manipulate objects.
  It's been so long since I read this book that the above isn't even
really a paraphrase, it's just what I remember of the discussion. One
excellent point he made is that some of these other "intelligences"
are more accurate predictors of success than sheer verbal and
calculating ability. 
  There are certainly a lot of people floating around who are very
good with words and numbers, but have a terrible time grasping the
subtleties of social interactions, for example.
   I for one am a person who can take any sort of multiple choice test
and score VERY highly, but I never felt that I was quite as smart as
some others who maybe test less well. I have  a terrible blind spot
with any sort of "visual" thinking and cannot learn chess, for
example, or tell whether one object will fit inside another. I also
have a hellish time understanding "flow charts" or data structure
diagrams unless all of the pieces are labelled with words. I'm a fairly
good programmer but I can never draw a picture of what I have done.
When I worked at a high-tech firm, I learned to communicate by writing
down in words what I did and then drawing a box around each word!
   I have another friend who I think is highly intelligent but who
tests at the borderline retarded level. He has a severe learning
disability; he reads and writes at a third grade level. He has to be
told things very slowly. He couldn't follow the presidential debates
on TV because they talked too fast, and he usually can only read
headlines. 
    I once explained a programming problem to him which our whole team
was struggling with, just because it was on my mind. It took him a
while to understand, as he had only a few weeks of Fortran (and
flunked it) but he came up with THREE solutions, only two of which had
occurred to my double-PhD boss! We tested his solution, too, and it
was partially correct. It was a problem in queuing theory applied to 
VMS performance statistics, with multiple queues and "lost" time. 
My point is that here's a guy who will never test at a high IQ but who
has great problem-solving abilities (very fast CPU; very slow I/O)


