> the population each generation. (I think each pair played
> several iterations before switching to a new opponant.) The GA
> always evolved into a stable tit-for-tat strategy when started
> with a random population.

I have to agree with Dale here.  "Tit for tat" doesn't even seem
clearly defined as a strategy in the situation we were originally considering
(based on freeway driving) where there is no history of interactions
when you go up against an opponent.  Tit for tat means to do whatever your
opponent did last time.  But if you've never seen this opponent before,
or even if you did you have no memory if it, you don't know what he did
last time.

This is really the classical public goods problem.  Whatever others
are doing, from always cooperate to always defect, you do better to
defect.  You can't be punished for your defection since no one will
remember when they are interacting with you again.  Dale has to be
right that "always defect" will win in this situation.

